}

drills.explanation:
The subclasses implement only the hooks; the base class owns the algorithm:

```java
static final class Savings extends Account {
    Savings(long balance) { super(balance); }
    @Override protected long fee(long cents) { return Math.max(50, cents / 100); }
}
static final class Checking extends Account {
    Checking(long balance) { super(balance); }
    @Override protected long fee(long cents) { return 0; }
    @Override protected long overdraftLimit() { return 10_000; }
}
```

Because `withdraw` is `final`, a subclass cannot "fix" one case by rewriting the whole method, and the tests calling it through an `Account` reference exercise the real polymorphic dispatch.
